+
+`eprintln!` panics when the write to stderr fails, and the binary that vendors
+this crate builds with `panic = "abort"` (`backend/Cargo.toml`), so a stderr
+consumer that has gone away turns every line printed from the startup and
+announce paths into an `abort()` and a core dump. Upstream never sees this: its
+own CLI owns stderr for the life of the process. A daemon started by a
+Quickshell that can be restarted underneath it does, and so does any invocation
+piped into a command that exits early.
+
+Each of the six calls now goes through a local `diag!` macro that writes with
+`writeln!(std::io::stderr(), ...)` and drops the error. The macro is defined in
+the same module, immediately after the `Result` alias, so the patch is confined
+to one file and one construct.
+
+Restoring `SIGPIPE` to `SIG_DFL` in the binary would have covered this crate
+without touching it, and was rejected: Rust ignores `SIGPIPE` at startup so that
+a write to a closed socket surfaces as `io::ErrorKind::BrokenPipe` instead of
+killing the process, and this crate writes to sockets whose peers disconnect
+mid-transfer. That trade swaps a rare crash for a common one.
+
+This entry is the only local change to the vendor that is not a protocol or
+security fix. It is a candidate to send upstream as-is: nothing about it is
+specific to Nearby.
